WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
- I don't use that phrase like,
"It saved my life", gingerly

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.170
but I mean, that suicide
hotline phone call,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.133
it saved my life.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.500
The story actually begins prior
to me calling the hotline.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.330
We use the word "crazy"
loosely a lot of the time

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:21.870
but I think the best way to describe

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.160
the feeling I was having
at the time was crazy.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:24.993
I was by myself.

00:00:24.993 --> 00:00:26.840
I was actually on my way
to pick up my daughter

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.160
at the time from daycare.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.500
Being on the bus and feeling crazy like,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.690
I wanted to take my clothes off,

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:36.300
I wanted to jump off the
bus, I wanted to harm myself.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.720
I can't call a suicide
hotline on the bus right now

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.130
'cause that's gonna look really crazy.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.790
So the best thing to do
would be to, I had known

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.480
that New York has like, I
forget what the number is,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.670
that where essentially you
can have a text conversation

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.580
to help you through the process

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.400
if you feel like you're
going to harm yourself.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.510
It was like, I'm trying to
text, I text the message.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.150
It took like a minute to get back

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.510
and it's like, "What is your problem?"

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.660
I was like, okay, I'm gonna
text you, robot person

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.170
on the other side, what my problem is.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.030
Okay, I don't know what
to do, I'm panicking.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.930
I'm like, okay do I run
across the street because

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.410
at this point, I'm trying
to figure out either,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.060
do I harm myself or do I not harm myself?

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.980
Like one or two, and I'm
like, okay well I need

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.720
to do something, so I
called the suicide hotline

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.520
and I can't remember the
exact, like how the phone call

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.460
began essentially, but
I remember the feeling

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.180
of the phone call and I
remember being affirmed

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.840
because I just felt overwhelmed.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.630
And between work, between
life, between fatherhood,

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.360
between not really
knowing what I was doing

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.070
and in that moment, it
felt like it was going

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.530
to be easier to not be here

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.850
and to essentially try to end my life.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:45.850
And to have a person kind
of listen to the stress

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.560
at the moment and say,
"It is okay, you're okay,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.660
wow this must be really hard for you,

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.940
this situation is really difficult.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.700
You're doing a fantastic job, though,

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.210
based on what you're telling me."

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.340
Listening without judgment, without bias,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.840
without prejudice, that
suicide hotline phone call

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.420
saved my life and I wish I
could remember the sister's name

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.380
who I spoke to because she was
so warm, she was so loving,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.690
she was so comforting.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.303
She sounded like a black auntie.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.060
Warm, the warmth was the word.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.360
There were no identifying
questions at all.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.210
She doesn't know my name.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.480
She doesn't know where I'm from.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.799
She didn't know where I was located.

00:02:22.799 --> 00:02:25.720
The conversation was directly related

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:26.910
to what I was going through

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.520
at that present moment and those feelings.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.810
She didn't ask me anything
else like, where are you,

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.330
or you know, none of that.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.650
Part of what she was doing was just

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.270
allowing me the space really to vent.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.330
It was less questions and more affirming.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.210
It was just something I needed
because there's that level

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.410
of shame you feel,
essentially, when you're trying

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.110
to tell a person that you care about

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.040
that I essentially wanna harm myself

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.900
because then it's very
easy, unfortunately,

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.600
for people who do know you,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.105
to start blaming themselves.

00:02:54.105 --> 00:02:58.130
I think, sometimes there's a fear, right?

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.990
Like, okay, do I make this phone call?

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.810
Am I essentially going to look crazy?

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.120
Is it crazy, does it even work?

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.270
And granted, I'm very much the
person that doesn't believe

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.120
in absolutes, so not
everything works for everybody.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.000
Sometimes maybe it's not the
hotline, maybe it's a relative,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.140
it's a friend, it's someone
you know you can call who's

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.890
going to essentially be
able to give you that space

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.423
and that freedom and that
room to give of yourself

00:03:23.423 --> 00:03:27.170
without concern of bias
or prejudice or judgment,

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.120
but the suicide hotline
did that for me, for sure.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.480
It's easy to kind of
stigmatize how a person looks

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.010
who might be suffering from some level

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.080
of like a mental health disorder

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.300
or a person who may be depressed.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.960
I don't look like the stereotypical person

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.500
who's been suffering from that.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.080
I'm also going through
this and processing this.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.350
I know you probably are too.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.710
I've kind of been blessed
and fortunate enough

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.580
to be given the opportunity
to have the language

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.550
and have the space to do
that, so I feel like it's kind

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.303
of my duty to kind of share
that with other people.

